hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  and book 11 of his City of God Saint  Augustine explores this notion of human  beings having been created after in the  image of God and this has raised a lot  of questions for many people who want to  interpret that passage from Genesis what  does it actually mean to be created in  the image of God does that mean that God  has fingers and a nose and you know hair  and stuff like that and Augustine  rejects anything like that instead he  says that the image of God within us is  in fact an image of the Trinity and he's  going to talk about this in terms of  what you might call basic aspects or  dimensions of our being and one of them  has to do with our very being in itself  he says we exist we have being and not  only do we exist that's one we also know  that we exist we have knowledge however  dim that knowledge may be we have some  self-consciousness we have some grasp on  our own existence I understand that I  exist and that maybe you don't and that  you aren't me and we could go on and on  he doesn't unpack this here but we might  adduce a lot of corollaries from this  about what it is to know our existence  and then he goes further yet and this is  where it becomes most interesting not  only do we exist not only do we know  that we exist we also love we have we  bear an affective relation at the very  core of our being we love our existence  and we also love the knowledge of that  existence and this is similar to how  many Trinitarian you could call them you  know metaphysicians or theologians  have an unpack this notion of the  Trinity you have the father you have the  son generated out of the father who is  in a certain sense the knowledge of the  father and then you have the Holy Spirit  who is the love between the father and  the son but a love that is you know  equally substantial or consubstantial or  however you want to put it and all three  of them are in fact and this is where it  gets a bit mysterious the same being and  you might say the relations within the  being and we're not going to worry about  that at this point because we're more  interested in the human being and the  corollaries for this so this is telling  us about what it is to to have a human  nature to exist to have knowledge of  existence and to love both that  existence and that knowledge now  Augustine goes a little bit further in  this and he says in these three things  no true seeming illusion disturbs us and  then there are some reasons one is that  he says no bodily sense is involved here  it's not disturbing us there's no as he  says representation or image or the  senses involved instead we grasp this as  he's going to say a little bit later on  through a higher sense that is  distinctively human he says there we go  we have another and far superior sense  belonging to the inner person by which  we perceive what things are just and  what unjust just by means of an  intelligible idea unjust by the means of  it right and this is this is the sense  that we're using - you might say make  sense out of what it is that we we are  so we're using a higher faculty and that  faculty now it could be deceived right  but it's not being deceived because of  the senses or some sort of sort of image  could I be deceived about this he brings  up the academics at this point and the  academics were skeptics they were people  who thought that for every position you  had to provide evidence for it if you  couldn't provide evidence or argument  for it then we should suspend belief in  it  and say well we don't really know and in  their view that applied to just about  everything well some of them accepted a  doctrine of probability put that aside  for the moment he says what if you're  deceived well if I am deceived I am I  exist because who's being deceived right  now if when you read this you say wait a  second I thought Descartes came up with  that idea  no actually Augustine talks about it in  the City of God and Descartes knew about  it through Augustine so Descartes is not  completely original in this respect and  he's pointing out that any deception  would be self contradictory if I'm  deceived about my own existence I must  exist in order to be deceived about it  but he goes further then he says there  we go  if I am deceived I am he who is not  cannot be deceived and if I am deceived  by the same token I am and since I am if  I am deceived how am i deceived in  believing that I am how can I be  deceived about having knowledge of my  own existence he says that it's certain  that if I am that I am if I am deceived  so I the person deceived would be even  if I received certainly I'm not deceived  in this knowledge that I am and  consequently neither am i deceived in  notice what he says here knowing that I  know we not only have knowledge of our  own existence we have knowledge of our  knowledge we we know that we know so we  can't be deceived about that either  he goes on further because remember  there is the third thing he says when I  love these two things I add to them a  certain third thing namely my love which  is of an equal moment and I cannot be  deceived in this that I love I know that  I love since in those things which I  love I am not deceived and then somebody  could say well what if you love the  wrong thing what if you love something  that isn't what you think it is like you  love somebody  who turns out to be a spy for a foreign  country right wouldn't that throw you  for a loop and you'd say well yes I'm  deceived about what they are but I'm not  deceived about the fact that I love them  I'm not deceived about the love and when  it comes to being loving towards our own  selves  I'm not deceived about the fact that I  exist I'm equally not deceived about the  fact that I love my own existence I  might love it in somewhat the wrong way  I might be in the dark about certain  aspects of my my being but I still do  know that I love myself and I love the  knowledge that I have of myself even if  sometimes it's quite painful says in  those things which I love I'm not  deceived even if those were false it  would still be true I loved false things  how can I just leave dand prohibited  from loving false things if it were  false that I love them since they are  true and real who doubts that when they  are loved the love of them is true and  real and Augustine goes further than  this in the chapter that immediately  follows that in book eleven and he talks  about plants and animals now they don't  have knowledge in the way that we do and  they don't possess that higher sense he  thinks the way that we do they are  dependent on the sense world or on what  they're able to take in perhaps through  plants through their you know  equivalent to senses the the biological  mechanisms that govern them but they  still do have being and even though they  don't have knowledge in the full sense  of their being they still do love their  being and desire their being they seek  out what is helpful for them and what  conduces to their continuing to exist  and they avoid what is going to be  damaging to them or cause them not to  exist he he goes on and he talks about  men and  in the similar sense we all try to avoid  our our own deaths but he says that  plants and shrubs which have no life as  enables them to shun destruction by  movements we can see they still seek in  their own fashion to conserve their  existence they root themselves more  deeply in the earth so they may draw  nourishment they draw out healthy  branches to the sky and then he goes out  a little bit further going down the  scale metaphysically in a lower state of  being and he says even lifeless bodies  which want not only some sensation but  even life they seek the upper air sink  deep or a balance so that they may  protect their existence in the situation  where they can exist most in accordance  of their nature we might not want to  follow Augustine that far we might see  that as just you know part of the what  it is for them to be that they don't  have any love for their being but he is  saying that this is something that  living beings do have and notice that  it's very important that for Augustine  we in fact love the knowledge of our  existence we want to understand what our  existence is we don't just want to exist  we want to understand things he says how  much human nature loves the knowledge of  its existence how it shrinks from being  deceived will be sufficiently understood  by this fact every person prefers to  grieve in a sane mind rather than to be  glad in madness and he says this is  unique to us this is grand and wonderful  instinct he says some of them have  keener eyesight than ourselves but they  cannot attain the spiritual light with  which our mind is somehow irradiated so  we can form right judgments of things  this doesn't mean of course that we have  it all figured out  that we totally understand our existence  that we love rightly in every case but  this is central to Augustine's  anthropology and what he's providing you  here with is a conception of what is at  the core of human nature understood not  just in terms of our being and 